   108	
   109	Run three identical copies of the service behind a load balancer, so requests
   110	are spread across them instead of queueing behind one.
   111	
   112	```yaml
   113	# docker-compose.yml
   114	services:
   115	  app1:
   116	    build: .
   117	    command: node server.js
   118	
   119	  app2:
   120	    build: .
   121	    command: node server.js
   122	
   123	  app3:
   124	    build: .
   125	    command: node server.js
   126	
   127	  caddy:
   128	    image: caddy:2
   129	    ports:
   130	      - "3000:3000"
   131	    volumes:
   132	      - ./Caddyfile:/etc/caddy/Caddyfile
   133	```
   134	
   135	```caddyfile
   136	# Caddyfile
   137	:3000 {
   138	  reverse_proxy app1:3000 app2:3000 app3:3000
   139	}
   140	```
   141	
   142	Run `docker compose up`, point `load.js` at the same `localhost:3000`, and the
   143	ten requests finish in roughly a third of the time, since up to three can be
   144	worked on at once.
   145	
   146	Now add a startup delay so a new replica is not treated as useful before it is
   147	ready, like a real service warming a cache or loading a model.
   148	
   149	```js
   150	// server.js
   151	import http from "node:http";
   152	
   153	let ready = false;
   154	const startupDelayMs = Number(process.env.STARTUP_DELAY_MS || 5000);
   155	setTimeout(() => {
   156	  ready = true;
   157	  console.log("Now ready");
   158	}, startupDelayMs);
   159	
   160	const simulateWork = () => {
   161	  const start = Date.now();
   162	  while (Date.now() - start < 300) {
   163	    // busy loop, stands in for real CPU-bound work
   164	  }
   165	};
   166	
   167	http
   168	  .createServer((req, res) => {
   169	    if (req.url === "/health") {
   170	      res.writeHead(ready ? 200 : 503);
   171	      res.end(ready ? "ready" : "not ready");
   172	      return;
   173	    }
   174	
   175	    if (!ready) {
   176	      res.writeHead(503, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });
   177	      res.end("not ready yet\n");
   178	      return;
   179	    }
   180	
   181	    simulateWork();
   182	    res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });
   183	    res.end("Handled by a replica\n");
   184	  })
   185	  .listen(3000, () => console.log("Listening on port 3000"));
   186	```
   187	
   188	Immediately after restarting, Caddy can otherwise forward traffic to a
   189	warming replica, causing `not ready yet` responses. Add active checks:
   190	
   191	```caddyfile
   192	:3000 {
   193	  reverse_proxy app1:3000 app2:3000 app3:3000 {
   194	    health_uri /health
   195	    health_interval 2s
   196	  }
   197	}
   198	```
   199	
   200	Caddy now polls `/health` every two seconds and sends real traffic only to
   201	ready replicas. In the first few seconds after `docker compose up`, before the
   202	first health check has run, Caddy may return an empty `503` itself because it
   203	has no replica marked healthy; that differs from a replica returning `not
   204	ready yet` and disappears within one `health_interval`.
   205	
   206	### Naming the pattern
   207	
   208	This is a replicated service: several identical, stateless copies of the same
   209	code, any of which can answer any request, behind a load balancer that
   210	distributes traffic across them. Stateless matters: the replicas share nothing
   211	like the workers in Unit 1, so they do not need a lock or shared volume to
   212	coordinate.
   213	
   214	The health endpoint is a **readiness probe**: it answers whether the service
   215	should receive traffic now, not merely whether its process is running. A
   216	service can be running but unready during startup. Production orchestrators

